{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mass.EquilateralBound","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mass.EquilateralBound","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.InnerProductSpace.PiL2","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Dimension.Finite","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":7,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.EquilateralBound.equidistant_card_le_three_in_R3","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.EquilateralBound.equidistant_card_le_three_in_R3","kind":"theorem","line":164,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/EquilateralBound.lean#L164-L178","doc":"**The arc's corollary: at most FOUR mutually equidistant centres in ℝ³.** Formally, at most three\npoints can sit at a common positive distance `d` from each other AND from a fourth point `p₀` — a\nfifth mutually-adjacent centre does not fit in three dimensions.\n\nConsequence for the α-cluster model: the complete α-graph with `N_bonds = C(n_α, 2)` presupposes that\nevery pair of α-centres is a nearest-neighbour bond at a common distance. That configuration is\ngeometrically unrealizable for `n_α ≥ 5`, so the complete-graph bond count is not merely inaccurate\nthere — it describes no configuration at all, and the coordination count must fall back to a\nclose-packing value. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.EquilateralBound.equidistant_card_le_finrank","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.EquilateralBound.equidistant_card_le_finrank","kind":"theorem","line":155,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/EquilateralBound.lean#L155-L162","doc":"**The geometric bound.** A family of pairwise-equidistant points (at positive distance `d`), all\nalso at distance `d` from a further point `p₀` outside the family, has at most `finrank` members —\nso a fully equidistant configuration has at most `finrank + 1` points in total. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.EquilateralBound.equilateralGram_of_dist","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.EquilateralBound.equilateralGram_of_dist","kind":"theorem","line":133,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/EquilateralBound.lean#L133-L153","doc":"The Gram condition holds for the difference vectors of pairwise-equidistant points. Uses the\npolarisation identity: `⟪x,y⟫ = (‖x‖² + ‖y‖² − ‖x−y‖²)/2`, which for `‖x‖ = ‖y‖ = ‖x−y‖ = d` gives\n`d²/2`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.EquilateralBound.card_le_finrank_of_equilateralGram","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.EquilateralBound.card_le_finrank_of_equilateralGram","kind":"theorem","line":126,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/EquilateralBound.lean#L126-L131","doc":"**At most `finrank` equilateral vectors.** Linear independence plus finite dimension. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.EquilateralBound.EquilateralGram.linearIndependent","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.EquilateralBound.EquilateralGram.linearIndependent","kind":"theorem","line":105,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/EquilateralBound.lean#L105-L124","doc":"**Equilateral vectors are linearly independent.** Immediate from the identity: a vanishing\ncombination forces `(Σcᵢ)² + Σcᵢ² = 0`, and both terms are non-negative, so every `cᵢ = 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.EquilateralBound.gram_inner_self","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.EquilateralBound.gram_inner_self","kind":"theorem","line":62,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/EquilateralBound.lean#L62-L103","doc":"**The key identity.** For an equilateral Gram configuration,\n`‖Σ cᵢ vᵢ‖² = (d²/2)·[(Σ cᵢ)² + Σ cᵢ²]`. Both bracketed terms are sums of squares, so the quadratic\nform is positive definite whenever `d ≠ 0` — this is the whole content of the bound, obtained without\ncomputing an eigenvalue. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.EquilateralBound.EquilateralGram","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.EquilateralBound.EquilateralGram","kind":"def","line":55,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/EquilateralBound.lean#L55-L60","doc":"**The equilateral Gram condition.** Vectors from a common apex to points that are pairwise at\ndistance `d`, and each at distance `d` from the apex, satisfy\n`⟪vᵢ, vⱼ⟫ = d²/2 + (d²/2)·δᵢⱼ` — the diagonal is `d²`, the off-diagonal `d²/2`. Writing both cases\nas one formula is what makes the identity below a two-line computation. ","signatureChunk":0}]}